\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Automatic mechanism for temple door actuation designed by Heron of Alexandria (62 CE). When the altar fire is lit, the air confined within cavity (E) undergoes thermal expansion, increasing the pneumatic pressure inside the water-filled reservoir (H). This pressure forces water through pipe (L) into the suspended cauldron (M). The resulting weight displacement overcomes the counterweight (R), thereby opening the doors. Once the fire is extinguished, pressure drops, and the system reverts to its equilibrium state. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {herondoor}.}}{2}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:herondoor}{{1.1}{2}{Automatic mechanism for temple door actuation designed by Heron of Alexandria (62 CE). When the altar fire is lit, the air confined within cavity (E) undergoes thermal expansion, increasing the pneumatic pressure inside the water-filled reservoir (H). This pressure forces water through pipe (L) into the suspended cauldron (M). The resulting weight displacement overcomes the counterweight (R), thereby opening the doors. Once the fire is extinguished, pressure drops, and the system reverts to its equilibrium state. Adapted from \cite {herondoor}}{figure.caption.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Seismoscope developed by Chinese polymath Zhang Heng (132 CE). A central pendulum suspended inside the bronze vessel oscillates in response to seismic vibrations. This displacement triggers an internal lever mechanism that opens the jaws of a specific dragon head, releasing a bronze ball into the mouth of a corresponding frog statue below. Eight radial dragon-frog pairs were meticulously aligned to determine the primary orientation of the earthquake epicenter. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {seismoscope}.}}{3}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:seismoscope}{{1.2}{3}{Seismoscope developed by Chinese polymath Zhang Heng (132 CE). A central pendulum suspended inside the bronze vessel oscillates in response to seismic vibrations. This displacement triggers an internal lever mechanism that opens the jaws of a specific dragon head, releasing a bronze ball into the mouth of a corresponding frog statue below. Eight radial dragon-frog pairs were meticulously aligned to determine the primary orientation of the earthquake epicenter. Adapted from \cite {seismoscope}}{figure.caption.7}{}}
